<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>literature</title><style>table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{border:1px solid #333;padding:6px}th{background:#eee}img{max-width:100%}</style></head><body><h1>literature</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Drama</li><li>Literary Devices</li><li>Poetry Analysis</li><li>Prose Analysis</li></ul><h2>Drama</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Drama?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Drama is a literary genre written to be performed on stage. It tells a story through dialogue and action rather than narration. In the NEB Class 11 English syllabus, drama includes both tragedy (serious drama with a disastrous ending) and comedy (humorous drama with a happy ending). Students are expected to understand dramatic elements such as plot, character, dialogue, stage directions, and thematic content. Drama analysis in NEB exams often involves answering comprehension questions about studied plays and writing critical appreciation of dramatic scenes.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Elements of Drama</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The key elements of drama are: (1) Plot — the sequence of events, including exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, and resolution. (2) Character — the people (or beings) who perform the action. (3) Dialogue — the spoken words between characters that reveal personality and advance the plot. (4) Stage directions — instructions written by the playwright about movement, expression, setting, and technical effects. (5) Theme — the central idea or message. (6) Setting — the time and place of the action. (7) Conflict — the struggle between opposing forces that drives the plot. (8) Resolution — the outcome of the conflict. Understanding these elements is essential for analysing any dramatic text in NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Types of Drama</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Drama is classified into several types: Tragedy — a serious drama dealing with a somber theme, usually involving the downfall of the protagonist due to a tragic flaw (hamartia). Example: Shakespeare&#x27;s &#x27;Macbeth&#x27;. Comedy — a light-hearted drama with a happy ending, often satirising human follies. Example: Shakespeare&#x27;s &#x27;The Merchant of Venice&#x27;. Tragicomedy — a blend of tragic and comic elements, where serious themes are treated with humour. Melodrama — exaggerated characters and sensational plots designed to elicit strong emotions. Farce — a comedic drama based on ridiculous situations and slapstick humour. Realism — drama that attempts to represent life truthfully, without supernatural or romantic elements. Symbolic drama — drama that uses symbols and allegory to convey deeper meanings.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Dramatic Devices</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Key dramatic devices include: Soliloquy — a character&#x27;s inner thoughts spoken aloud when alone on stage (e.g., Hamlet&#x27;s &#x27;To be or not to be&#x27;). Asides — brief remarks spoken by a character to the audience that other characters cannot hear. Monologue — a long speech by one character addressed to other characters on stage. Dramatic irony — when the audience knows something the characters do not, creating tension or humour. Stage business — actions performed by actors that are not part of the dialogue (e.g., pacing, handling props). Curtain — a division of a play into acts and scenes. Prologue — an introductory speech or passage that sets the scene or introduces themes. Epilogue — a concluding speech or passage that provides resolution or commentary. These devices are frequently tested in NEB drama questions.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Character Types in Drama</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Dramatic characters can be classified as: Protagonist — the main character around whom the plot revolves. In tragedy, this is the hero whose downfall is the focus. Antagonist — the character or force that opposes the protagonist. Tragic hero — a noble protagonist with a fatal flaw (hamartia) that leads to their downfall. Foil — a character who contrasts with the protagonist to highlight particular qualities. Stock character — a stereotypical character representing a common type (e.g., the wise old man, the comic servant). Round character — a complex, multi-dimensional character capable of change. Flat character — a simple character with limited traits. In NEB exams, students may be asked to analyse characters in studied plays using these classifications.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Structure of a Play</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Plays are structured in acts and scenes . A act is a major division of a play (commonly three or five acts in classical drama). A scene is a subdivision of an act, usually marked by a change in setting or the entrance/exit of characters. The five-act structure (exposition, rising action, climax, falling action, resolution) was popularised by the Roman dramatist Horace and is used in many classical and modern plays. However, many modern plays use a two-act or one-act structure. Understanding the structure helps students answer questions about the progression of events and the development of conflict in a play.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Soliloquy vs. Monologue: A soliloquy is a speech where a character speaks their inner thoughts aloud when ALONE on stage (e.g., Hamlet&#x27;s &#x27;To be or not to be&#x27;). A monologue is a long speech by one character addressed to OTHER CHARACTERS on stage (e.g., a character explaining their feelings to another). The key difference is the audience: soliloquy has no other characters present; monologue has listeners on stage.</td></tr><tr><td>Tragedy vs. Melodrama: Tragedy deals with serious themes and the downfall of a noble protagonist due to a tragic flaw — it evokes pity and fear (catharsis). Melodrama uses exaggerated characters, sensational plots, and clear moral distinctions between good and evil — it evokes strong emotions but not catharsis. Tragedy is literary and complex; melodrama is sensational and straightforward. NEB exams test understanding of tragedy, particularly the concept of the tragic hero and hamartia.</td></tr><tr><td>Protagonist vs. Antagonist: The protagonist is the MAIN CHARACTER around whom the plot revolves — often (but not always) the hero. The antagonist is the character or force that OPPOSES the protagonist. In some plays, the antagonist is the villain; in others, it is an abstract force (fate, society, nature). Understanding the relationship between protagonist and antagonist is essential for analysing dramatic conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Dramatic Irony vs. Situational Irony: Dramatic irony occurs when the AUDIENCE knows something the CHARACTERS do not (e.g., in Romeo and Juliet, the audience knows Juliet is not really dead, but Romeo does not). Situational irony occurs when the OUTCOME is opposite to what was EXPECTED (e.g., a fire station burning down). Both involve a contrast between expectation and reality, but dramatic irony depends on the audience&#x27;s superior knowledge, while situational irony depends on the unexpected outcome of events.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Literary Devices</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Are Literary Devices?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Literary devices are techniques that writers use to convey meaning, create effects, and enhance the aesthetic quality of their writing. They go beyond literal language to produce deeper meanings, vivid imagery, and emotional impact. In the NEB Class 11 English syllabus, literary devices are tested through identification, explanation, and application in both prose and poetry passages. Mastery of these devices is essential for literary analysis and comprehension answers.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Metaphor</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>A metaphor is a direct comparison between two unlike things without using &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27;. It asserts that one thing IS another. Examples: &#x27;All the world&#x27;s a stage&#x27; (Shakespeare — the world is compared to a stage). &#x27;Time is a thief&#x27; (time steals moments from our lives). &#x27;Her voice was music to his ears&#x27; (her voice is compared to music). Metaphors create vivid imagery by linking two unrelated ideas, forcing the reader to see one thing in terms of another. Unlike similes, metaphors are more direct and powerful because they state the comparison as fact.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Simile</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>A simile is a comparison between two unlike things using &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27;. Examples: &#x27;Her smile was like sunshine&#x27; (her smile is compared to sunshine using &#x27;like&#x27;). &#x27;He runs as fast as a cheetah&#x27; (his speed is compared to a cheetah using &#x27;as&#x27;). &#x27;As brave as a lion&#x27; (bravery compared to a lion&#x27;s courage). Similes are more explicit than metaphors because they signal the comparison with &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27;. They help readers visualise qualities by connecting them to familiar images. In NEB exams, distinguishing simile from metaphor is a common question.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Personification</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Personification is giving human qualities, emotions, or actions to non-human things (animals, objects, ideas, natural forces). Examples: &#x27;The wind whispered through the trees&#x27; (wind cannot literally whisper). &#x27;The sun smiled down on the valley&#x27; (the sun cannot literally smile). &#x27;Opportunity knocked on the door&#x27; (opportunity cannot literally knock). &#x27;The mountains stood like sentinels&#x27; (mountains given human role). Personification brings non-living things to life, making descriptions more vivid and emotionally resonant. It is one of the most frequently identified literary devices in NEB comprehension passages.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Imagery</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Imagery is descriptive language that appeals to the five senses: sight, sound, smell, taste, and touch. Visual imagery (sight): &#x27;The golden sunset painted the sky in shades of orange and pink.&#x27; Auditory imagery (sound): &#x27;The rain tapped a rhythmic beat against the windowpane.&#x27; Olfactory imagery (smell): &#x27;The aroma of freshly baked bread filled the kitchen.&#x27; Gustatory imagery (taste): &#x27;The lemon was so sour that her lips pucker&#x27;d.&#x27; Tactile imagery (touch): &#x27;The rough bark scratched his palms.&#x27; Imagery creates vivid mental pictures and sensory experiences, immersing the reader in the text. Identifying the type of imagery used is a common NEB question.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Alliteration</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Alliteration is the repetition of the same initial consonant sound in closely connected words. Examples: &#x27;The fair field filled with fan&#x27; (alliterative f-sound). &#x27;Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers.&#x27; &#x27;The wild wind whistled through the wires.&#x27; Alliteration creates rhythm, musicality, and emphasis. It is commonly used in poetry, slogans, and brand names (&#x27;Coca-Cola&#x27;, &#x27;PayPal&#x27;). In NEB exams, alliteration is identified by noting the repeated consonant sounds at the beginning of words in a phrase or sentence. Note: it is the sound, not just the letter, that matters — &#x27;ph&#x27; in &#x27;phone&#x27; and &#x27;philosophy&#x27; creates alliteration even though the letters differ.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Metaphor vs. Simile: A simile compares using &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27; (&#x27;Her smile is like sunshine&#x27;). A metaphor states a direct comparison without &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27; (&#x27;Her smile is sunshine&#x27;). Both compare two unlike things, but the metaphor is more direct and powerful. In NEB exams, confusing these two is a common error — always check for &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27; to identify a simile.</td></tr><tr><td>Personification vs. Anthropomorphism: Personification gives human qualities to non-human things (abstract ideas, objects, nature): &#x27;The wind whispered.&#x27; Anthropomorphism gives human form or behaviour to animals or objects in a more literal sense: &#x27;The cat wore a hat and spoke English.&#x27; Personification is a figure of speech; anthropomorphism is often used in fables and children&#x27;s stories where animals literally act like humans.</td></tr><tr><td>Hyperbole vs. Exaggeration: Hyperbole IS deliberate exaggeration for effect — the two terms are often used interchangeably. However, &#x27;hyperbole&#x27; is the literary term for intentional, recognised exaggeration, while &#x27;exaggeration&#x27; can refer to any overstatement, intentional or not. In NEB exams, use &#x27;hyperbole&#x27; when identifying the literary device.</td></tr><tr><td>Irony vs. Coincidence: Irony involves a contrast between expectation and reality — there is often an element of intention or meaningful reversal. Coincidence is a chance occurrence of events that seem related but have no causal connection. &#x27;It&#x27;s ironic that the fire station burned down&#x27; (expected safety, unexpected destruction). &#x27;It was a coincidence that we met at the airport&#x27; (chance event, no ironic contrast). Students often confuse these in comprehension questions.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Poetry Analysis</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Poetry Analysis?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Poetry analysis is the careful examination of a poem&#x27;s meaning, structure, language, and literary devices. In the NEB Class 11 English syllabus, students study selected poems and are expected to understand their themes, appreciate their literary techniques, and answer questions about meaning and effect. Poetry analysis requires reading beyond the literal words to understand the poet&#x27;s message, mood, and artistic choices. A good analysis addresses: theme, tone, imagery, literary devices, structure, and the poet&#x27;s purpose.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Understanding Theme in Poetry</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>The theme of a poem is its central idea or message about life, human nature, or society. Unlike a topic (which is a subject — &#x27;love&#x27;, &#x27;nature&#x27;, &#x27;death&#x27;), a theme is a complete statement about that subject. For example, the topic &#x27;love&#x27; has the theme &#x27;Love is a powerful force that can bring both joy and suffering.&#x27; To identify the theme: (1) Read the poem carefully, noting repeated ideas or images. (2) Consider the poet&#x27;s attitude toward the subject. (3) Look for the poem&#x27;s resolution or final statement. (4) Express the theme as a universal statement, not just a summary of events. Common themes in NEB poetry include: love, nature, death, time, hope, social justice, and human relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Identifying Tone and Mood</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Tone is the poet&#x27;s attitude toward the subject, revealed through word choice, imagery, and rhythm. Mood (or atmosphere) is the emotional feeling evoked in the reader. Common poetic tones: lyrical (expressing emotion), narrative (telling a story), dramatic (conversational, often with conflict), reflective (contemplative, philosophical). Common moods: melancholy, joyful, somber, optimistic, ironic, nostalgic, fearful. To identify tone and mood: examine the poet&#x27;s word choices (positive/negative connotations), the poem&#x27;s rhythm and pace, and the overall emotional effect. In NEB exams, students are often asked: &#x27;What is the tone of the poem?&#x27; or &#x27;How does the poet create mood?&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Imagery in Poetry</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Imagery in poetry uses sensory language to create vivid mental pictures. Types of imagery: Visual (sight): &#x27;The golden sunlight filtered through the leaves.&#x27; Auditory (sound): &#x27;The distant thunder rumbled across the valley.&#x27; Tactile (touch): &#x27;The rough bark scraped against my palm.&#x27; Olfactory (smell): &#x27;The sweet perfume of jasmine drifted through the night.&#x27; Gustatory (taste): &#x27;The bitter taste of failure lingered on his tongue.&#x27; Poetry analysis requires identifying the types of imagery used and explaining how they contribute to the poem&#x27;s meaning and emotional effect. Imagery is the primary tool poets use to make abstract ideas concrete and sensory.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Literary Devices in Poetry</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Common literary devices in poetry: Metaphor — direct comparison without &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27;. Simile — comparison using &#x27;like&#x27; or &#x27;as&#x27;. Personification — giving human qualities to non-human things. Alliteration — repetition of initial consonant sounds. Assonance — repetition of vowel sounds. Consonance — repetition of consonant sounds (not necessarily at the beginning). Onomatopoeia — words that imitate sounds (buzz, crash, whisper). Repetition — repeating words or phrases for emphasis. Apostrophe — addressing an absent person or abstract idea directly. Rhetorical question — a question not expecting an answer. Enjambment — a sentence continuing beyond the end of a line of poetry. Caesura — a pause within a line of poetry (marked by punctuation). Identifying and explaining these devices is a core skill in poetry analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Poetic Structure and Form</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Poems have various structures and forms . Stanza — a grouped set of lines (like a paragraph in prose). Verse — a single line of poetry. Rhyme scheme — the pattern of rhymes at the end of lines (ABAB, AABB, ABCB). Meter — the rhythmic pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables (iambic pentameter = five pairs of unstressed-stressed syllables). Free verse — poetry without regular meter or rhyme. Sonnet — a 14-line poem with a specific rhyme scheme. Haiku — a three-line Japanese form (5-7-5 syllables). Ballad — a narrative poem, often with a repetitive structure. In NEB exams, students should be able to identify the form of a poem and explain how its structure contributes to its meaning.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Poet vs. Speaker: The poet is the real person who wrote the poem (e.g., William Wordsworth). The speaker is the voice or persona within the poem (e.g., the &#x27;I&#x27; who &#x27;wandered lonely as a cloud&#x27;). They are not always the same person. In NEB exams, attributing the speaker&#x27;s views directly to the poet without qualification is a common error.</td></tr><tr><td>Theme vs. Topic: The topic is the subject of the poem (e.g., &#x27;nature&#x27;, &#x27;love&#x27;, &#x27;death&#x27;). The theme is what the poem says about that subject — a complete statement (e.g., &#x27;Nature has the power to heal the human spirit and restore inner peace&#x27;). A theme is always a complete sentence; a topic is a single word or short phrase.</td></tr><tr><td>Tone vs. Mood: Tone is the poet&#x27;s attitude toward the subject (e.g., ironic, reverent, melancholy). Mood is the emotional atmosphere felt by the reader (e.g., tense, peaceful, ominous). Tone is created by the poet&#x27;s word choices; mood is the reader&#x27;s experience. A poet may have an ironic tone while creating a tense mood. These are related but distinct concepts.</td></tr><tr><td>Metaphor vs. Symbol: A metaphor is a direct comparison (A is B): &#x27;All the world&#x27;s a stage.&#x27; A symbol is an object that represents something beyond itself: &#x27;The white whale in Moby-Dick symbolises the elusive nature of truth.&#x27; A metaphor is a figure of speech; a symbol is a recurring image or object that carries deeper meaning throughout a work. Every metaphor can be read symbolically, but not every symbol is a metaphor.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Prose Analysis</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>What Is Prose Analysis?</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Prose analysis is the examination of non-poetic written works — including short stories, essays, excerpts from novels, and speeches — to understand their meaning, structure, and literary techniques. In the NEB Class 11 English syllabus, prose analysis covers both fictional prose (short stories, novel excerpts) and non-fictional prose (essays, speeches, narratives). Students are expected to analyse characters, themes, setting, narrative technique, and the author&#x27;s purpose. Prose analysis is tested through comprehension questions, critical appreciation, and essay-type responses in NEB exams.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Analyzing Characters in Prose</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Character analysis involves examining: Physical description — how the character looks (reveals personality through appearance). Dialogue — what the character says (reveals thoughts, education, attitude). Action — what the character does (reveals true character through behaviour). Thoughts — what the character thinks (direct insight into motivation). Reaction of others — how other characters respond (reveals the character&#x27;s impact). Characters can be round (complex, developed, capable of change) or flat (simple, one-dimensional). Characters can be dynamic (undergo significant change) or static (remain essentially the same). In NEB exams, character sketches should include specific textual evidence to support each trait described.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Understanding Setting in Prose</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Setting is the time and place in which a story occurs. Setting analysis involves: Physical setting — where the story takes place (city, village, forest, room). Temporal setting — when the story takes place (historical period, season, time of day). Social setting — the cultural and social environment (class structure, customs, beliefs). Psychological setting — the emotional atmosphere that reflects characters&#x27; inner states. Setting is not merely backdrop — it often influences plot, shapes characters, and reinforces themes. For example, a stormy night may create tension and mirror a character&#x27;s turmoil. In NEB exams, setting questions ask students to identify the setting and explain its significance to the story.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Identifying Theme in Prose</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>The theme of a prose work is its central message or insight about life. To identify theme: (1) Look for recurring ideas, images, or symbols throughout the work. (2) Consider the conflict and its resolution — what does the outcome suggest? (3) Examine character development — what does the character learn or fail to learn? (4) Consider the title — it often hints at the theme. Express the theme as a complete, universal statement: &#x27;Love requires sacrifice&#x27; (theme) vs. &#x27;Love&#x27; (topic). Common prose themes include: good vs. evil, coming of age, social injustice, the power of knowledge, the conflict between tradition and modernity, human nature, and moral choices. NEB exams frequently ask: &#x27;What is the theme of the story?&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Narrative Techniques in Prose</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Narrative techniques are the methods authors use to tell a story. Key techniques: Point of view — who tells the story (first person &#x27;I&#x27;, third person limited, third person omniscient). Flashback — interrupting the present narrative to show past events. Foreshadowing — hints or clues about future events. Flashforward — jumping ahead to show future events. Irony — contrast between expectation and reality (verbal, situational, dramatic). Symbolism — objects or actions representing abstract ideas. Dialogue — conversation between characters that reveals personality and advances plot. Internal monologue — a character&#x27;s inner thoughts presented directly. In NEB exams, students should be able to identify these techniques and explain how they contribute to the story&#x27;s meaning and effect.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Plot Structure in Prose</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>The plot is the sequence of events in a story, typically following this structure: Exposition — introduction of characters, setting, and basic situation. Rising action — events that build tension and develop the central conflict. Climax — the turning point or moment of highest tension. Falling action — events that follow the climax and lead toward resolution. Resolution (denouement) — the conclusion where conflicts are resolved. Some stories have open endings (leaving the resolution ambiguous) or circular endings (returning to the beginning). Understanding plot structure helps students answer questions about the story&#x27;s development, the significance of specific events, and the author&#x27;s narrative choices.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Plot vs. Story: The story is the complete sequence of events in chronological order (what happened). The plot is the specific arrangement of those events as presented by the author (how the author chose to tell it). A story might be told out of order (using flashbacks), but the plot is what the reader actually experiences. For example, in &#x27;The Gift of the Magi&#x27; by O. Henry, the story includes the couple&#x27;s poverty, their gifts, the sacrifices — but the plot reveals the ending first (the sold hair and watch chain) and then flashes back to show how each sacrifice occurred, creating dramatic irony.</td></tr><tr><td>Theme vs. Moral: A theme is the central idea or insight about life that a work explores — it is implicit and complex. A moral is an explicit lesson or instruction — usually a simple statement of right and wrong. Fables and parables typically have morals (&#x27;Honesty is the best policy&#x27;). Literary short stories and novels typically have themes (&#x27;The corruption of power destroys the individual&#x27;). In NEB exams, always express theme as a complete, nuanced statement — never simply name it as a single word.</td></tr><tr><td>Setting vs. Context: Setting is the specific time and place of a narrative (19th-century London, a rainy afternoon in a village). Context is the broader historical, cultural, and social background that shapes the work&#x27;s meaning (Victorian social norms, the Industrial Revolution&#x27;s impact on rural communities). Setting is part of the story&#x27;s world; context is the real-world background that informs the story. In NEB exams, setting questions focus on the story&#x27;s world; context questions require knowledge of the historical period.</td></tr><tr><td>Round vs. Complex: A round character is well-developed with multiple traits, capable of surprise, and psychologically realistic. A complex character is similar but may also involve contradictions or moral ambiguity. All round characters are complex, but not all complex characters are round (a flat character can have one complex trait). In NEB exams, &#x27;round&#x27; is the more commonly tested term. A round character example: Ebenezer Scrooge (changes from miserly to generous). A flat character example: a stereotypical villain who exists only to oppose the hero.</td></tr></table><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Drama</td><td>Drama covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Literary Devices</td><td>Literary Devices covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Poetry Analysis</td><td>Poetry Analysis covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Prose Analysis</td><td>Prose Analysis covers essential principles and applications.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Literature.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Close reading builds an interpretation from precise details—word choice, syntax, imagery, sound, form and structure. A label such as ‘metaphor’ or ‘alliteration’ is only a starting observation; explain its effect in this particular passage.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Support claims with accurate, located evidence and distinguish observation from interpretation. When two readings fit, explain evidence for each and acknowledge counterevidence; do not state authorial intention as fact without a source.</p><h3>Open research question — How evidence supports more than one reading</h3><p>Literary interpretation is evidence-based rather than a hunt for one hidden answer. A defensible open reading offers at least two text-supported interpretations, cites exact details, and explains what complicates each; authorial intention should not be asserted without evidence.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>In “Literature”, which two interpretations can the exact wording support, and what detail complicates each? Record the line or passage; leave the issue open if the text does not decide it.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://www.york.ac.uk/english/about/writing-at-york/writing-resources/close-reading/">University of York: close reading and ambiguity</a></li><li><a href="https://writing.wisc.edu/handbook/academicwriting/closereading/">UW–Madison: close reading</a></li><li><a href="https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/general_writing/academic_writing/rhetorical_situation/elements_of_rhetorical_situations.html">Purdue OWL: rhetorical situation</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>